      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"unaided memory\" language is notoriously risky and could potentially be interpreted to cover any idea that entered your brain during access, even if you infused it with your own unique skills or later context. While courts generally distinguish between prior existing knowledge and ideas inspired by exposure, proving you \"remembered\" proprietary specifics is difficult, and you might lose if your new code looks suspiciously similar to what you learned. To stay safe, you should document how you independently arrived at that pattern before working on their project, while strictly avoiding direct copying of their logic or data structures.",
